          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I had flu two weeks ago and I am still exhausted. Is prolonged tiredness afterwards a concern, and what does guidance describe about antivirals and vaccination?",
          "answer": "Prolonged tiredness after influenza can be a common experience, referred to as post-viral fatigue. This can occur even after initial recovery from flu symptoms. However, if tiredness persists or worsens, it's advisable to discuss this with a clinician to rule out any underlying complications, as the guidance indicates that influenza may lead to worsening of existing conditions or other complications.\n\nRegarding antiviral treatment for flu, it is most effective when started within 48 hours of symptom onset. Early treatment is recommended for those who are hospitalized, have severe illnesses, or are at higher risk for complications. For otherwise healthy individuals who seek care within this timeframe, antiviral treatment could still be considered, but it is associated with only a modest reduction in symptom duration (source: \"Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment\").\n\nVaccination against influenza is also recommended as a preventive measure, particularly for people at higher risk due to underlying conditions. Annual influenza vaccination is seen not just as a respiratory health measure but also as beneficial for overall health, particularly in the context of cardiovascular disease (source: \"Core cardiovascular prevention measures\"). \n\nI could not provide specific treatment or management plans, as well as personal health advice tailored to your situation. Please consult a healthcare provider for tailored guidance.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
